WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.223
The tube's canal is full of fluid.

2
00:00:06.163 --> 00:00:07.823
Where is it made, and where does it go?

3
00:00:08.783 --> 00:00:12.883
In each ventricle, the choroid plexus secretes cerebrospinal fluid.

4
00:00:13.803 --> 00:00:15.803
It is the main source, not the only one.

5
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:21.573
The net flow runs one way: from the lateral ventricles to the third, then through

6
00:00:21.573 --> 00:00:22.853
the aqueduct into the fourth.

7
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:25.693
The exit is in the hindbrain.

8
00:00:26.653 --> 00:00:30.333
Openings in the fourth ventricle let fluid out around the brain and cord.

9
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:36.607
In most adult humans, the cord's own central canal is largely closed and is not a

10
00:00:36.607 --> 00:00:39.987
major route. The fluid circulates around the cord instead.

11
00:00:41.017 --> 00:00:45.237
On the usual teaching numbers, the fluid is replaced about three times a day.

12
00:00:46.137 --> 00:00:47.517
Is it ever completely fresh?

13
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.427
Picture a glass of dyed water.

14
00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:54.127
Pour in clean water slowly, stirring as it overflows.

15
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:58.487
After one full glassful, the dye has faded but not gone.

16
00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:02.357
About 37% % of the dye is still there.

17
00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:05.117
Replacing a volume does not empty it.

18
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.560
Now plug the cerebral aqueduct while secretion continues.

19
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:11.480
Which ventricles swell?

20
00:01:17.483 --> 00:01:20.983
Everything upstream: both lateral ventricles and the third.

21
00:01:21.903 --> 00:01:23.703
Not the fourth, downstream of the plug.

22
00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:30.593
Now the seal. In muscle, for example, a protein tracer in the blood leaks out of the

23
00:01:30.593 --> 00:01:31.593
capillaries.

24
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:35.927
In the brain's cortex, it goes no farther than the cells lining them.

25
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:41.887
Three layers could be the seal: the endothelial cells, the basement membrane around

26
00:01:41.887 --> 00:01:44.027
them, and astrocyte endfeet outside.

27
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:51.300
In 1967, Thomas Reese and Morris Karnovsky injected horseradish peroxidase into

28
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:57.420
mice. Wherever this enzyme lies, a chemical reaction leaves a dense deposit an

29
00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.940
electron microscope can see.

30
00:01:59.883 --> 00:02:01.463
Which layer stopped the tracer?

31
00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:08.943
The endothelial cells.

32
00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:12.083
The deposit filled the vessel and went no farther.

33
00:02:12.983 --> 00:02:14.223
That holds for this tracer.

34
00:02:15.217 --> 00:02:18.517
Tight junctions between those cells were the probable block.

35
00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:23.667
Then Milton Brightman and Reese put the tracer into the ventricles instead.

36
00:02:24.717 --> 00:02:29.657
It spread between brain cells, reached the capillaries from outside, and stopped at

37
00:02:29.657 --> 00:02:30.657
the same junctions.

38
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:35.800
Two experiments, run in opposite directions, pointed to the same layer.

39
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.260
A second seal sits on a specialized part of the tube's lining.

40
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.320
In the choroid plexus, leaky capillaries lie under a sealed epithelium.

41
00:02:46.283 --> 00:02:48.623
So the net flow runs one way through the tube.

42
00:02:49.423 --> 00:02:53.043
Two main seals, both selective, decide what gets in from the blood.
